From 6d8002d7562cfa40f567b62d5420c2525826e927 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:13:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge: Dolby Vision sent as HDR10+, the statistics
 and the scenes

A display that takes HDR10+ and not Dolby Vision (many Samsung TVs) gets a
Dolby Vision video rebuilt as plain HDR10, with no dynamic metadata at all.
HDR10+ can carry what it lacks: per scene, the average and the distribution
of maxRGB (SMPTE ST 2094-40). This is the part that decides what is sent;
it holds no GL, so a host test runs it.

- The scenes are the stream's own: a picture starts a scene when its level 1
  block is not that of the picture before, or when its scene refresh flag is
  set and the one before was not (a stream that sets it on every picture has
  its scenes in level 1). Nothing is detected in the picture.
- What is measured of a picture is the maxRGB of its samples (a histogram of
  4096 bins in PQ and the sum in cd/m2). A scene's statistics are those of all
  of its pictures measured so far, pooled; the first picture of a scene goes
  out with the metadata of the scene before, because a picture is measured
  only after it is drawn (nothing waits for the GPU) and nothing is made up
  for a scene not yet measured.
- Within a scene the metadata change only when a value of the InfoFrame (16
  cd/m2 units) would change and the value itself has moved by more than 32
  codes of 4095 in PQ: the display's mapping is not moved by every picture,
  nor by a value that wavers at the edge of a code.
- What is sent: application version 1, one window, the luminance parameters,
  no tone mapping curve and a targeted system display maximum luminance of 0,
  which is profile A (hdr10plus_tool, MIT, read as data, refuses profile A
  with any other value). The entries at 5 and 10 % carry 0 and 0.00255, as
  Hades and the fork of CroqueMr read ST 2094-40 8.5.4 (not verified: the
  specification is not read, and the HDR10+ metadata that FFmpeg's FATE and
  hdr10plus_tool test with carry other values there).
- The film's own area is measured: the active area of the level 5 block, read
  as dvbridge_dv_margins() reads it (as offsets of the picture, and as those
  of a 3840x2160 master it was cropped or scaled from).
- A measurement of a scene that has ended is dropped; with no measurement for
  48 pictures nothing is sent (the frames go out as HDR10) until one comes.

Measured on Cosmos Laundromat (Netflix Open Content, CC BY 4.0; 17 520
pictures, the 100 shots of its Dolby Vision XML), against the ideal of each
scene with all of its pictures known, from every pixel, in float64, in
InfoFrame codes over the eight values sent:
- this: 2.92 codes off on average, 51 changes within a scene per 1000
  pictures, p99.98 off by 17.7 % on average;
- each picture measured on its own (no scenes, the picture waiting for the
  GPU, as the fork of CroqueMr does, its 16 cd/m2 bins): 4.73 codes, 539
  changes, 31.4 %;
- from level 1 alone: 29.4 codes.
Sampling 960 columns gives the same as every pixel (2.92 against 2.92).
